  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1147 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nThe upper-level low pressure previously over Minnesota has dug \ndeeper into Illinois. This is paired a continued high pressure \nsystem over the southwestern CONUS. Nearer to the surface, high \npressure continues to reside and keep skies clear with fairly \nlight winds. Tomorrow is forecast to warm back up towards triple\ndigit highs with the warmest areas in the far western portions \nof Kansas. Light winds and pleasant weather will accompany the \nearly August heat.\n\nMonday is forecast to be the hottest day this week with highs near \nthe Colorado border up to 108 degrees. Highest areas of apparent \ntemperatures in those areas are around 104/105 degrees... With 105 \nbeing the criteria for a Heat Advisory, it will need to be monitored \nclosely. Whether or not it meets precise criteria, it will be hot \nand traditional heat precautions are strongly advised. \nHydration and cooling breaks are encourage not just Monday, but \nevery day this week with the summer heat. \n\nTuesday onward sees continued heat and occasional periods of \nslight (10-25%) probability chances. Tuesday will start cooler \nback in the 90s, but through Saturday are expected to be of \nsimilar temperatures, warming about a degree per day with ending\naround 100- 105 degrees. Especially Tuesday and Wednesday will \nsee the highest precipitation chances in the \nwestern/northwestern counties and near the Colorado border. \nEnsemble percentages reach as high as 30%. Still a healthy \namount of uncertainty remains in both scope and strength, but \nthe current expectation is if there is a severe threat it will \nbe very limited.",
